course: dictionary
lesson: 3563
title: Ferry: a boat and a job
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a thing or describe an action. Ferry does both: a ferry is a boat, and to ferry is to carry people or things across water, often many times. Look at the clues around the word to see which one is meant. In «Cars drive onto a ferry's big decks», cars drive onto something, so the ferry must be the boat.
explain: Look for the other clue words too. «Take the ferry», «ferry terminal» and «ferry across» all point to a boat on a route. A terminal is the place where boats start and stop, so you can guess it is where you wait to board. Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: "a boat that carries cars across the sea" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Safety clues tell you more. «Passengers keep to the passenger decks and listen to safety announcements» shows that a ferry carries passengers, and the announcements tell them where the life jackets are. Clues like these help you understand a word even when you have never been on a ferry.
fact: A ferry is a boat or ship that carries people, cars and goods across a river, lake or sea.
fact: A ferry runs on a set route, going to and fro between the same places.
fact: To ferry means to carry people or things from one place to another, often many times.
fact: Ferry can be a noun (a ferry, ferries) or a verb (to ferry, ferried, ferrying).
fact: «Dreich» (Scotland) means dull, grey and wet, and «wee» means little, so «a wee bit dreich» means a bit of miserable weather.
exercise: «Cars drive onto the ferry's big decks.» What is a ferry? | *A boat or ship that carries cars and people across water | A road that goes up a hill | A bridge over a river | A kind of car
exercise: What does to ferry mean? | To paint a boat | To swim across a lake alone | *To carry people or things from one place to another, often many times | To stop cars at a gate
exercise: «There was no bridge and no ferry-boat, so he hopped.» Why did he have to hop across the river? | Because the ferry was too big to get on | Because he wanted to see the water | *Because there was no bridge or ferry to cross the river | Because he was playing a game
test: «Passengers keep to the passenger decks and listen to safety announcements.» Why do passengers listen on a ferry? | Because the announcements are funny | So they can sing with the crew | *So they know what to do to stay safe
test: «A wee bit dreich» in a storm means terrible weather, so the ferry is probably what? | Painted red | Faster than usual | *Cancelled
exam: Which sentence uses ferry as a noun? | «They ferried the cows across the field.» | «Mum will ferry us to the swimming pool.» | *«The ferry was late because of the storm.»
